//- 5:10 JWST. A spaceship with its own sensors, translated for us. This plants the spaceship the denouement pays off.

//- Hubble and Webb side by side, Webb on the click. Each half is an img in the slide, cropped to the pillars.
section.over-media.pillars(data-background-color="#000" data-timing="30")
  img.hubble(src="img/pillars-hubble.jpg" alt="The Pillars of Creation in visible light, from Hubble")
  img.webb.fragment.fade-in(data-fragment-index="1" src="img/pillars-webb.jpg" alt="The Pillars of Creation in infrared, from Webb")
  p.label.hubble Hubble
  p.label.webb.fragment.fade-in(data-fragment-index="1") Webb
  cite.credit NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI
  aside.notes
    ul
      li The Pillars of Creation. Hubble, in light like the light you see: colors you've seen.
      li Click: Webb, in infrared. Nobody has ever seen these colors, including the people who made the picture.

section.over-media.translated(data-timing="20")
  +webbTranslation
  aside.notes
    ul
      li You see the sliver on the left. Everything Webb sees is infrared, all of that dark band, and none of it has a color.
      li So the image team gives each filter a visible color in wavelength order. Shortest to purple, longest to red. For the Pillars: purple, blue, cyan, yellow, orange, red, from 0.9 to 4.7 micrometres. NASA calls it representative color.
      li Yes, the shortest one is purple. The color that doesn't exist.
      li They do on purpose what your brain does by accident.
      li First to cut after the vote if the yes path runs long. Keep the spaceship.

section.over-media(data-background-image="img/webb-mirror.jpg" data-timing="15")
  h2 Every spaceship has its own sensors.
  cite.credit Photo: NASA
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Webb's mirror, the day it opened in the cleanroom.
      li Every spaceship has its own sensors. This one sees light no human can, and translates it for us.
      li Land "spaceship" clearly. The next slide hangs off it, just like you, and it comes back in the denouement.
